    "spans": [
     {
      "end": 977,
      "start": 0,
      "text": "Effects of Age, Sex, and Ethnicity on the Association Between Apolipoprotein E Genotype and Alzheimer Disease: A Meta-analysis\n\u2014Among Caucasian subjects from clinic- or autopsy-based studies, the risk of AD was significantly increased for people with genotypes \u22082/\u22084 (OR=2.6, 95% Cl=1.6-4.0), \u22083/\u22084 (OR=3.2, 95% Cl=2.8-3.8), and \u22084/\u22084 (OR=14.9, 95% CI=10.8-20.6); whereas, the ORs were decreased for people with genotypes \u22082/\u22082 (OR=0.6, 95% Cl=0.2-2.0) and \u22082/\u22083 (OR=0.6, 95% Cl=0.5-0.8). TheAPOE\u22084-AD association was weaker among African Americans and Hispanics, but there was significant heterogeneity in ORs among studies of African Americans (P Conclusions. \u2014TheAPOE\u22084 allele represents a major risk factor for AD in all ethnic groups studied, across all ages between 40 and 90 years, and in both men and women. The association betweenAPOE\u22084 and AD in African Americans requires clarification, and the attenuated effect ofAPOE\u22084 in Hispanics should be investigated further."
     }
    ],
